    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'French Dominoes',
            subtitle: 'The Caribbean Cross Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "French Dominoes is a cut-throat domino game from the Caribbean (particularly Jamaica) featuring a distinctive cross-shaped tile layout. Played with a double-six domino set, it requires players to form a cross pattern with tiles before extending outward, combining careful strategy with luck. The game emphasizes blocking opponents while emptying one's own hand.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'French Dominoes originated in the Caribbean, particularly Jamaica, despite its European name. It likely developed from European domino traditions adapted to Caribbean gaming culture.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Exactly 4 players, competing individually (cut-throat style).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard double-six domino set (28 tiles/bones).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dominoes, paper and pencil for scoring.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Shuffle all 28 dominoes face-down. Each player draws 7 dominoes and places them in their rack. Remaining 0 dominoes stay as the draw pile.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Alternative Zero Scoring: The double-zero domino counts as 10 points instead of 0, preventing anomalies where a player with only a blank tile in hand wins the round scoring 0.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Casual Rules (No Pass Penalty): Three consecutive passes do not incur a 10-point penalty; the game simply passes back to the first player.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Two-Player French Dominoes: Each player draws 12-13 dominoes. Play proceeds similarly but with only two competitors. Winning scores and blocking rules may be adjusted.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
