          equipment: 'Dominoes, paper and pencil for scoring.',
          dealing: 'Shuffle all 28 dominoes face-down. Each player draws 7 dominoes and places them in their rack. Remaining 0 dominoes stay as the draw pile.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Be the first player to empty your hand, or have the lowest pip count if the game becomes blocked. Players earn points from opponents' remaining dominoes; first to 100 points is eliminated; lowest-scoring player among survivors wins.",
          gameplay: "1. **Opening Round**: The player holding the double-zero (0-0) must play it ('pose' it). 2. **Subsequent Rounds**: The previous winner plays any double or passes. 3. **Cross Formation**: Four bones with blank ends must be played on the four sides of the initial double before other plays extend outward. 4. **Turn Play**: Players clockwise must play a matching domino (or draw if unable). 5. **Extending Dominoes**: Once a double is played on each arm, players can extend those sequences with matching-end dominoes. 6. **Ending**: Round ends when one player empties hand or all players pass consecutively.",
          keyRules: [
            'The initial domino must be a double, creating four extension arms.',
            'Blanks (0-pip dominoes) must be played against the four sides of the center double before other tiles.',
            "Unplayed doubles count double toward the player's score.",
            "Winner's final double multiplies other players' scores by 3 (or 4 if they hold unplayed doubles).",
            'Three consecutive passes incur a 10-point penalty.',
            'Illegal plays or passing when able incurs 10-point penalty.',
            'Some variations treat the double-zero tile as worth 10 points instead of 0 to prevent anomalies.',
            'Play direction: Anti-clockwise (counter-clockwise).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Try to play blocking dominoes early. Keep high-value dominoes (doubles) in hand as long as possible. Pay attention to which numbers have been played to know what tiles may be stuck in other players' hands. Always try to play blanks strategically on the cross to control the game.",
          intermediate: 'Track opponent hands: Count remaining tiles and estimate their likely dominoes. Manage your own doubles carefullyâ€”holding unplayed doubles increases your penalty if you lose. Play defensively by blocking numbers opponents may need. Bluff by playing in patterns that suggest you have certain dominoes.',
          advanced: 'Positional blocking: Play tiles that force opponents to draw multiple times, depleting the draw pile. Domino counting: Memorize all played dominoes to calculate exact probabilities of draws. Control the cross: By playing strategic dominoes on the center cross, limit the extensions available. Endgame calculation: When the game is nearly blocked, calculate whether finishing first or tying/blocking for lowest score is optimal.',
          tips: [
            'In early rounds, play freely to control the cross. In late rounds, be conservative with your tiles.',
            'High doubles (double-six and double-five) are valuableâ€”hold them to block opponents unless you can play them without consequence.',
            'If an opponent is close to emptying their hand, consider blocking aggressively rather than extending plays.',
            'The blank tile is your most flexible tool; save blank-matching dominoes for crucial moments.',
            "Pay attention to pip totals: If you're behind in score and near 100, prioritize emptying your hand over blocking.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
